CORE
main.cpp

^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ pointer to integer: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ pointer to unrelated pointer: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ cast away const: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [expr.type.conv]/1.1: with a single expression, T(e) is equivalent to
the cast expression (T)e, which [expr.cast]/4 may interpret as a
reinterpret_cast or a const_cast.  When T was a typedef-name the front end
resolved T(e) as constructor overload resolution of a pseudo-constructor
that admits only implicit conversions ("found no match for symbol 'U'",
bin53).  libstdc++'s __waiter_pool_base::_S_for computes `uintptr_t(__addr)'
(bits/atomic_wait.h:252).  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this program
(-std=c++98), and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
